                              Agile Methodology and Scrum

1. Agile Methodology
Agile is a software development approach based on flexibility and collaboration.
It divides work into small, manageable parts.
It encourages continuous testing and customer feedback.
It delivers working software frequently.
It allows teams to adapt to changing requirements.

2. Scrum
Scrum is an Agile framework used to manage software development.
It divides work into fixed periods called Sprints.
It defines three accountabilities: Product Owner, Scrum Master, and Developers.
It uses events such as Sprint Planning, Daily Scrum, and Sprint Review.
It helps teams collaborate and improve continuously.

3. Sprint
A Sprint is a fixed period during which the team works toward a goal.
A Sprint usually lasts 1–4 weeks.
The team selects tasks during Sprint Planning.
Developers build and test working product increments.
Each Sprint ends with a review and retrospective.

4. Iteration
An iteration is a repeated cycle of development.
The team plans, develops, and tests features.
Each iteration improves the product.
Feedback is used to make changes.
Iterations help deliver software incrementally.

5. User Story
A user story describes a feature from the user's perspective.
It explains what the user needs and why.
It is usually written in simple language.
Common format: “As a user, I want to log in so that I can access my account.”
It includes acceptance criteria to clarify expected behavior.

6. Epic
An epic is a large body of work.
It represents a major feature or business requirement.
It is too large to complete easily in a single Sprint.
It can be divided into smaller user stories.
Example: An online shopping system.

7. Story Point
A story point estimates the relative effort required for a user story.
It considers complexity, effort, and uncertainty.
Teams may use values such as 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, and 13.
Story points are not the same as hours.
They help teams estimate and plan Sprint work.

8. Acceptance Criteria
Acceptance criteria define the conditions a feature must satisfy.
They clarify what is expected from a user story.
They help developers understand requirements.
Testers use them to verify the feature.
Example: A user can log in with valid credentials.


Scrum Roles- Responsibilities

9.Product Owner (PO)
Defines and communicates the product vision.
Manages and prioritizes the Product Backlog.
Understands customer and business requirements.
Clarifies user stories and acceptance criteria.
Maximizes the value delivered by the product.

10. Scrum Master (SM)
Helps the team understand and follow Scrum.
Facilitates Scrum events when needed.
Helps remove impediments affecting team progress.
Coaches the team in self-management and continuous improvement.
Helps the organization understand Scrum practices.

11. Developers
Design and develop product features.
Write and maintain application code.
Test features and fix defects.
Collaborate to achieve the Sprint Goal.
Deliver a usable product increment that meets the Definition of Done.

12. Stakeholder
Represents an interested party, such as a customer or business team.
Shares requirements and expectations.
Provides feedback on product functionality.
Participates in discussions and reviews when appropriate.
Helps the Product Owner understand business needs.

Scrum Event and Terms- Purpose

13. Sprint Planning
Takes place at the beginning of a Sprint.
The team discusses why the Sprint is valuable.
The team selects work from the Product Backlog.
Developers plan how to complete the selected work.
The team establishes a Sprint Goal.

14. Daily Scrum
A short, daily event lasting 15 minutes.
Developers inspect progress toward the Sprint Goal.
They coordinate their work for the next day.
They identify obstacles affecting progress.
They adjust their plan when necessary.

15. Sprint Review
Takes place at the end of a Sprint.
The team presents or inspects the completed product increment.
Stakeholders provide feedback.
The team discusses progress toward product goals.
The Product Backlog may be updated based on feedback.

16. Sprint Retrospective
Takes place after the Sprint Review and before the next Sprint Planning.
The team reflects on how the Sprint went.
It identifies what worked well.
It discusses problems and possible improvements.
The team plans ways to improve quality and effectiveness.

17. Product Backlog
An ordered list of work needed to improve the product.
It may contain features, bug fixes, and technical improvements.
The Product Owner is accountable for effective backlog management.
Items are refined and prioritized as needed.
It changes as requirements and business needs evolve.

18. Sprint Backlog
Contains the Sprint Goal.
Includes Product Backlog items selected for the Sprint.
Includes the Developers' plan for delivering the work.
Developers update it as they learn more during the Sprint.
It helps the team track and manage Sprint work.

19. Sprint Goal
Describes the main objective of a Sprint.
Provides direction and focus for the team.
Helps the team coordinate different tasks.
Allows flexibility in the exact work needed to achieve the goal.
Should deliver meaningful value toward the product.

20. Definition of Done (DoD)
Defines the shared quality standard for a completed product increment.
Ensures work meets the team's agreed requirements.
May require code review and successful automated tests.
May include documentation and integration checks.
Work is considered done only when it meets the applicable Definition of Done.



